### Actual automatic knot identities

`knotmpc.f:176–284, 292–407` creates a separate dummy SPC node for a native
expanded receiving node. Each generated directional equation receives a
`KNOT1`, `KNOT2` or `KNOT3` label and a corresponding type-R prescribed term.
The equation identifies the expanded receiver, original translational
reference, generated rotational and expansion nodes, and dummy term.
One/three-dimensional cloud rows contain seven terms; planar cloud rows
contain nine, including a deliberate zero/duplicate expansion term.

These type-R rows are not the same construction as an explicit RBE's
`rigidmpc.f` rows. The current `bs_record_rigid` hook therefore cannot
authenticate automatic knots. A new observer must retain identities at the
actual `knotmpc` branch, validate the complete generated graph, and distinguish
those algebraic values from physical world supports. Accepting every KNOT
label or every R-type SPC would not establish that distinction.

`gen3delem.f:414–425` fills the newly generated knot coefficients once the
expanded coordinates exist. The catalogue must be inspected after that
native step. The observer must tolerate the native representation, including
its expansion terms, rather than replace it with a hand-written rigid-joint
model.

### Physical rotations and projected support values

`gen3dboun.f:110–123` transfers an original rotational prescription on an
existing knot to its generated rotational node. Its non-knot static branch
instead creates the existing mean-rotation construction near the current
`bs_record_angle` seam. The first specimen's guided trunk root is a one-beam
endpoint; its internal fork remains a separate automatic knot. A second
component requires a second genuine root mapping, not another alias to the
first support's helper nodes.

`resultsforc.c` observes the native equation multiplier and distributes its
coefficient contributions before the projected SPC vector is final. An
authenticated knot dummy value may be retained for audit, but it is excluded
from the physical force/moment sum. Physical translations contribute force
at the original application node; prescribed physical rotations contribute
couples about their declared axes. The current boundary index, node,
direction, type and actual prescription must remain bound to the native row.
Finite round-off in a converged value is distinct from a nonzero authored
prescription, as already established for V4.

### Mapper geometry and moment reference

`map3dto1d2d.f:328–335` supplies section geometry from `co + vold`, averaging
the native section corners for its centroid. The force and centroid-relative
moment increments already exist at the current observer seam. They must be
copied with their original element/end identity and literal basis.

`linstatic.c:810–836` calls retained `results` before copying solved `v` into
`vold`. For the first zero-initial, single linear step, reference-state mapper
geometry is an explicit specimen expectation that can be checked separately.
The general output convention remains `CO_PLUS_NATIVE_MAP_VOLD_ARGUMENT`;
it must not be renamed as guaranteed final displaced geometry.

For an origin-wrench comparison, the oracle can transport the observed native
centroid-relative moment by the observed centroid and independently check the
force against fixed source statics. The origin target is independently derived
from declared load positions; it is not recovered from beam stresses or fitted
to a beam-tip displacement. A disagreement of geometry conventions must not
be hidden by altering the benchmark limit.

## Proposed bounded network seam

The separate V5 investigation uses count-based source, support and spring
catalogues and the unchanged native element/end mapper. Each beam component
is a connected acyclic branching B32R component with straight centered
elements, zero-offset rectangular sections, and one uniform material/section
per component. Different components may have genuinely different assignments.
One physical zero guide per component leaves the common scalar translation
free. A single grounded SPRING1 and an acyclic set of real SPRING2 connections
form the connected scalar support path. SPRING2 endpoints must lie on their
actual common global force axis; coordinate coincidence does not substitute
for original node identity.

The first vertical slice has one T, one grounded scalar connection and two
distinct point sources. The next proposed slice connects two genuine T
components with one internal SPRING2 and retains one ground spring and two
physical guides. This avoids inventing a second redundant ground path solely
to satisfy a catalogue. Source and support wrenches, individual actual spring
ends, typed knot algebraic rows, element/section/material identities and the
single physical frame remain separate observations.

These are source-supported implementation seams and proposed safeguards.
Runtime qualification and numerical budgets belong to the independent oracle
and coordinator's retained validation. Rotational springs, arbitrary releases,
RBE beam receiving sets, contact, nonlinear laws and Oomakhi design acceptance
are not established by this proposal. Native mechanics and ordinary output
remain unchanged throughout the investigation.
